<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Right breast cancer, stage IIA, after bilateral mastectomy and axillary dissection (C50.121)</content>. No evidence of recurrence. Dr. Cho agrees that tamoxifen alone is the right adjuvant treatment and that chemotherapy and radiation would add little at his age with one involved node. [S]<list><item>Continue the plan from the consultation.</item><item>Germline result to be reviewed with the genetic counselor.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Long-term tamoxifen; bone health (Z79.810)</content>. Tolerating tamoxifen with mild hot flashes. DXA shows low bone mass at the femoral neck (T-score -1.2) and a normal spine (-0.4); vitamin D was 24 ng/mL. His ten-year fracture risk is below the treatment threshold, so no bone drug is needed. [S]<list><item>Cholecalciferol 1000 units daily, starting today.</item><item>Calcium from food, about 1200 mg a day; walking and light weights.</item><item>Recheck vitamin D in three months; repeat DXA in two years.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Cancer-related history and examination of both sides of the chest wall and both axillae</td><td>Every 3 to 6 months for 3 years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No nodule, skin change, or nodes</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Lymphedema assessment and risk-reduction counseling</td><td>Every visit after axillary surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Arm circumferences equal at 31 cm; skin care and early reporting reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Venous thromboembolism symptoms on tamoxifen</td><td>Every visit on tamoxifen</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No leg swelling or pain, chest pain, or breathlessness</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Hot flashes on tamoxifen</td><td>Every visit on tamoxifen</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild, at night; no treatment needed. Paroxetine, fluoxetine, and bupropion are to be avoided because they block the activation of tamoxifen</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Sexual side effects of tamoxifen (libido, erectile function)</td><td>Every visit on tamoxifen</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not asked today, a deviation from the protocol; the visit was taken up by the bone density and consultation results. To be asked at the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence to tamoxifen</td><td>Every visit on tamoxifen</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses; pill bottle kept by his toothbrush</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Distress and depression screen (PHQ-2)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 score 0</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Bone density (DXA)</td><td>Baseline on hormone therapy, then every 2 years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Femoral neck T-score -1.2 (low bone mass); lumbar spine -0.4 (normal)</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>At baseline, then three months after starting a supplement</td><td>Due</td><td>24 ng/mL at the first visit; recheck in three months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Germline multigene panel testing</td><td>Once, for every man with breast cancer</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Blood sent today by medical oncology; genetic counseling before and after the result</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Medical oncology</td><td>Once, then as the oncologist advises</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Breast imaging (mammography)</td><td>Yearly only for a remaining breast after lumpectomy, or the other breast with a predisposing variant</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Both breasts were removed; there is no breast tissue to image</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Routine blood tests, tumor markers, and CT, bone scan, or PET</td><td>Not recommended without symptoms in stage I to III disease</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No symptoms suggesting recurrence</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Physical activity</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Walking most days; light weights added for bone health</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Take tamoxifen every day with side effects managed, aiming to continue for at least five years. In progress; target 2026-08-11 [S]</item><item>Complete germline genetic testing with counseling, and give him written information for his relatives. In progress; target 2026-05-12 [S]</item><item>Establish bone health on hormone therapy at his age: DXA, vitamin D of 30 ng/mL or more, and weight-bearing exercise. In progress; target 2026-05-12 [S]</item><item>Give a written survivorship care plan with the surveillance schedule. In progress; target 2026-08-11 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Hassett MJ, et al. Management of Male Breast Cancer: ASCO Guideline. J Clin Oncol 2020;38(16):1849-1863 (2020); NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Breast Cancer, Version 5.2025 (2025); Shapiro CL, et al. Management of Osteoporosis in Survivors of Adult Cancers With Nonmetastatic Disease: ASCO Clinical Practice Guideline (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, breast cancer monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Primary care review in three months with a vitamin D recheck. Genetic counseling appointment for the result. Medical oncology every six months. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-12: three-month follow-up: germline result, tamoxifen side effects and adherence, vitamin D recheck. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
